{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CghedM3pN22MTnXaHpDce","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can't give reliable values for Dq and B from these data, so I'm not reporting any. I did not run a fit. My reasoning for declining is below.\n\n1. **Many possible assignments.** The ground state of low-spin d⁵ is t₂g⁵ (²T₂g). It is the only term of that configuration, so every spin-allowed d–d band is a t₂g→e_g excitation to a doublet of t₂g⁴e_g¹. That configuration gives about ten doublets: two ²T₁g, two ²T₂g, two ²E_g, one ²A₁g and one ²A₂g. Their energies depend on 10Dq together with B and C through configuration-interaction mixing. Three bands could be matched to these states in several ways, and each assignment gives different Dq and B. Nothing in the data picks one. The 29 360 and 29 620 cm⁻¹ bands are only 260 cm⁻¹ apart, so which two states they correspond to is especially ambiguous.\n\n2. **Charge-transfer and spin-orbit effects.** Low-spin d⁵ complexes are typically 4d or 5d ions, or cyanide- or π-acceptor-ligated 3d ions. In these complexes ligand-to-metal charge-transfer bands lie in the same near-UV region as the doublet d–d bands. Large spin-orbit coupling also mixes the doublets with the lower quartets, so the \"spin-allowed\" label is only approximate. The observed maxima may therefore not be pure ligand-field transitions. A Dq/B fit that treats them as pure doublet–doublet transitions would be unreliable.\n\n3. **Weak constraint on B.** With C/B fixed at 4.4, the band positions give one absolute energy scale, which mainly sets 10Dq, and two energy differences. B is fixed only by those two differences, which depend on the assignment (point 1) and on transitions that may not be pure d–d (point 2). The model is therefore poorly determined.\n\nI have not given Dq or B because they cannot be determined reliably within the ligand-field model.\n\n{\"Dq\": null, \"B\": null, \"C\": null, \"out